{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787077346,"nonce":"Fu5BsiaGetKYr+sM5kQiZN3tsSc=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABNQwVUw","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/114","origin":"github"},"title":"r-adaptivity: relocate() writes back a mesh that is not the geometry it validated (2.4x error gap)","message":"`jno.solve.relocate()` writes back a mesh that is not the geometry it validated. A solve after the\nrelocate loop, and a fresh `jno.fem` built on `fem.domain.mesh.points`, both give an error **2.4x\nlarger** than the number the loop's own path reports -- and on the suite's headline r-adaptivity\nproblem the relocated mesh is then *worse* than the uniform one it is asserted to beat.\n\n### How it surfaced\n\n`FEM.solve` snapshotted `self._op` on entry and restored it in a `finally` unconditionally, though\nonly `basis=` ever swaps it. That reverted the rebinding every `adapt=` driver performs on the way\nout, so a **second** `fem.solve()` after an adaptive run silently dispatched on the pre-adapt\noperator. Fixed under \"restore only what this call swapped\".\n\n`test_relocate_beats_a_uniform_mesh_on_an_underresolved_front` calls `fem.solve()` a second time (in\nits `final_err` helper) and so was measuring relocation through the operator that predates the\nrelocation. With the restore corrected it fails:\n\n    relocation should beat a uniform mesh here: 1.141e-01 vs 9.379e-02\n\n### The measurement\n\nAllen-Cahn front, `eps=0.03`, `size=0.06`, `relocate(lr=5e-3, max_iters=30)`; final-time relative\nL2 against `tanh((x-0.5)/(sqrt(2) eps))`:\n\n| path | error | ratio vs uniform |\n|---|---|---|\n| uniform mesh | 9.379221e-02 | 1.000 |\n| re-solve through the **rebound** operator | 1.141326e-01 | 1.217 |\n| **fresh `jno.fem` built on the relocated points** | 1.141326e-01 | 1.217 |\n| what the test asserted (pre-adapt operator) | ~4.8e-02 | ~0.51 |\n\nMax node motion 6.74e-02, i.e. larger than the mesh size -- the relocation is substantial, not noise.\n\nThe first two agree **to 15+ digits**, which is the load-bearing part: the operator the driver leaves\non the caller's FEM and an independent build on `fem.domain.mesh.points` describe the same discrete\nproblem. So the final operator and the written-back mesh are consistent with each other, and\n*inconsistent* with whatever geometry the loop scored 0.51 on.\n\nThat rules out the obvious explanation (the trainable coordinate parameter falling back to its seed):\nthat would reproduce the uniform number, and it does not. It is also not \"the stale operator is just\nthe uniform mesh\" -- then `err_moved == err_uniform` and the `\u003c 0.75 *` assertion would have failed\ntoo. The pre-adapt operator is solving on a third geometry, better than both.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nWhatever the loop optimises, `fem.domain` is what a user inspects, plots, exports and re-solves on.\nToday those disagree, and the disagreement is the size of the effect r-adaptivity is supposed to buy.\nEither the driver writes back the wrong iterate, or the coordinate parameter and `mesh.points`\ndiverge during the loop and only one of them is carried out.\n\n### Consequences to check\n\n* The sweep recorded in that test's docstring (ratios 4.99 / 0.96 / 0.51 across `eps = 0.15 / 0.06 /\n  0.03`) was produced through the same path and should be re-measured before it is quoted again.\n* `test_the_two_objectives_each_win_on_their_own_problem` compares the two objectives through the\n  same helper and is likely measuring the same artifact.\n* If the written-back mesh is the honest one, then r-adaptivity does not win on this problem and the\n  claim needs restating rather than the test needs fixing.\n\nMarked `xfail(strict=True)` rather than relaxed, so it reports the day the geometries agree.","files":null}]}